@page "/exsheet/functions"
@using global::ExSheet.Engine

@* ExSheet's functions (ADR-0110, ADR-0047), a reference page: every function the engine supports, from its
   own list, DeclaredFunction.All, grouped by the categories of the catalogue the build read. Its sections are
   the categories, which the rail lists. The filter is C#. A function the catalogue lists and the engine does not
   support yet links to the Roadmap section that holds its ticket. *@
<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Functions"
          Lead="Every function ExSheet's engine supports, by category, with its arguments as Excel writes them. Each gives Excel's result; a function outside this list is #NAME?, never an approximation, and where Excel has it, no Formula catches that #NAME?.">
    <ReferenceAsOf>
        The functions and their arguments are the engine's own list, <code>DeclaredFunction.All</code>; the categories are
        its catalogue's.
    </ReferenceAsOf>

    <div class="docs-functions-filter">
        <MudTextField T="string" @bind-Value="_filter" Immediate="true" Label="Filter" Placeholder="SUM, text, date…"
                      Variant="Variant.Outlined" Margin="Margin.Dense" Clearable="true" Adornment="Adornment.Start"
                      AdornmentIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.FilterList" aria-label="Filter the functions" />
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="docs-muted docs-functions-count">@CountText</MudText>
    </div>
    <p class="docs-muted">
        Where the engine cannot give Excel's answer for an argument, it gives an Error Value rather than a different
        answer: see <a href="exsheet/formulas#supported-functions">Formulas</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-muted">
        The functions a platform's math library would answer (EXP, LN, LOG, LOG10, POWER, the trigonometric and the
        hyperbolic functions) are computed by the engine itself, so a workbook shows the same last digit in every browser
        and on the server, where each library rounds a few arguments its own way. EXP, LN, LOG10, ASIN, ACOS, ATAN, ATAN2,
        SINH, COSH, TANH, ASINH, ACOSH, ATANH and a whole POWER give the exact value rounded once to the nearest double;
        SIN, COS and TAN reduce the angle as Excel does, and the others, a POWER that is not whole among them, are
        computed from those values.
    </p>

    @foreach (var (category, functions) in FunctionCatalogue.Supported)
    {
        var shown = functions.Where(f => FunctionCatalogue.Matches(f, _filter)).ToList();
        @if (shown.Count > 0)
        {
            <FunctionTable Title="@category.Name" Anchor="@category.Anchor" Functions="shown" />
        }
    }
    @if (FunctionCatalogue.Uncatalogued.Where(f => FunctionCatalogue.Matches(f, _filter)).ToList() is { Count: > 0 } uncatalogued)
    {
        <FunctionTable Title="@FunctionCatalogue.UncataloguedTitle" Anchor="@DocsAnchor.Of(FunctionCatalogue.UncataloguedTitle)" Functions="uncatalogued" />
    }
    @if (Matching == 0)
    {
        <p class="docs-notes-none">No supported function's name or description holds “@_filter”.</p>
    }

    @if (FunctionCatalogue.Pending.Count > 0)
    {
        <section class="docs-section" id="@PendingAnchor">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" HtmlTag="h2" Class="docs-section-title">
                <a href="@($"{FunctionCatalogue.Route}#{PendingAnchor}")" class="docs-anchor">@FunctionCatalogue.PendingTitle</a>
            </MudText>
            <p>
                Excel functions the catalogue lists, which the engine does not answer yet: in a Formula each is
                <code>#NAME?</code> today, which no Formula catches: <code>=IFERROR(NPV(0.1,A1:A3),0)</code> is not
                0, and a cell that reads such a Formula is <code>#VALUE!</code>. Each links the place on the Roadmap
                that holds the work on it.
            </p>
            <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table docs-reference-table">
                <thead><tr><th style="width: 22%">Function</th><th style="width: 30%">Category</th><th>On the Roadmap</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var function in FunctionCatalogue.Pending)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>@function.Name</code></td>
                            <td>@function.Category</td>
                            <td><a href="@function.RoadmapHref">@function.RoadmapTitle</a></td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </MudSimpleTable>
        </section>
    }
</DocsPage>

@code {
    private static readonly string PendingAnchor = DocsAnchor.Of(FunctionCatalogue.PendingTitle);

    private string? _filter;

    private int Matching => DeclaredFunction.All.Count(f => FunctionCatalogue.Matches(f, _filter));

    private string CountText => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_filter)
        ? $"{DeclaredFunction.All.Count} functions supported"
        : $"{Matching} of {DeclaredFunction.All.Count} functions";
}
